cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this paper, please cite it as below."
title: "Per-Provider Effects in Open-Weights LLM Routing: OpenRouter Is Null for Closed-Weights but Multi-Provider for Open-Weights"
abstract: >
  We test the common methodological assumption that the access route
  (direct vendor API, OpenRouter, or other aggregator) does not
  systematically affect measured behavior of frontier large language
  models. The picture splits along a structural axis: closed-weights
  models route only through the lab itself (direct-vs-OpenRouter is
  null), while open-weights models route across a marketplace of
  third-party hosts. Per-provider pinning across every multi-upstream
  open-weights model in the v2 Convergent Form, Divergent Voice corpus
  surfaces three structurally distinct categories of provider-layer
  effect: a large within-model deployment outlier (Google Vertex's
  MiniMax M2 deployment, 3.4x baseline composite; eight Bonferroni-
  surviving within-OR pairs across the six-cell M2 family, exactly
  every Google-pinned cell against every non-Google-pinned cell,
  with |d| from 0.57 to 0.75; replicates eight days later at
  within-Google d=0.15 (n.s.) and same-day fresh within-OR
  contrast d=0.73, p<10^-7), a smaller within-model deployment effect
  (Kimi K2-thinking AtlasCloud-vs-Google, d=0.40, p_Bonf=0.005), and
  a routing-layer integrity pathology (DekaLLM's GLM 4.7 endpoint
  returning prompt-keyed cached responses). The remaining
  open-weights ladders are null under multiple-comparisons
  correction. The methodological consequence: cross-lab studies using
  OpenRouter for open-weights models should pin upstreams via
  provider.only and additionally inspect per-cell latency and
  response-uniqueness signatures, not because most upstreams differ
  but because rare per-provider effects of all three kinds exist and
  cannot be predicted in advance from public metadata.
